\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The counting square is the Laplacian}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Keep the torus and the coboundary of \ref{fdip-00OK}. The
counting pairing is the sum #{\langle\alpha,\beta\rangle
=\sum_c\alpha(c)\beta(c)} over all cubes. There is a unique
linear map #{\extdag}, of degree #{-1}, such that
#{\langle \covwedge\alpha,\beta\rangle=\langle\alpha,\extdag\beta\rangle}
for every cochain. Set #{\covsq=-(\covwedge\extdag+\extdag\covwedge)} and
#{\extD=\covwedge+\extdag}. Then #{\extD^2=-\covsq}.}

\p{The marks are those of \ref{fdip-00PV}. The operator #{\extdag}
is the counting adjoint of #{\covwedge}, and under a positive
inner product it equals minus the contraction. That sign is where
the adjoint convention leaves the algebra. The operator #{\extD}
stays because the square is self-adjoint. The positive operator
#{\Delta} equals #{-\covsq}.}

\proof{
\p{The module of cochains is finite and free on the cubes. Take
those cubes as an ordered basis. The counting pairing is the dot
product of the coordinates, so a cochain orthogonal to every
cochain is zero, and the adjoint of #{\covwedge} is the transpose of its
matrix. The transpose of #{\covwedge^2} is #{(\extdag)^2}.
\ref{fdip-00OK} gives #{\covwedge^2=0}, hence #{(\extdag)^2=0}. The
cross terms in #{\extD^2} are #{\covwedge\extdag+\extdag \covwedge}.}
}
